{
  "stages": {
    "prototype": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "The goal is to learn fast whether an idea is worth pursuing, and the code is meant to be thrown away, so reading every line wastes the main benefit. Keep it in a sandbox: no real user data, no production credentials, and no quiet promotion into the real codebase."
    },
    "proof-of-concept": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "Demos have to work reliably in front of an audience and often outlive their intended lifespan, so skim the core paths and run the demo scenario end to end. Be honest with stakeholders about what is real and what is held together with tape, and plan to rewrite rather than harden it."
    },
    "mvp": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "Real users mean real data, so read every line of security-sensitive code (auth, payments, data handling, permissions) and back the rest with automated tests on the key user flows. Selective review keeps you fast while someone on the team still understands the architecture you will build on."
    },
    "production": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "A team has to maintain, debug, and extend this code for years, so every change needs a reviewer who understands it and will own it, just like human-written code. AI can still write most of the code; what it can't replace is human accountability and shared understanding of the codebase."
    },
    "critical": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "AI-written code is acceptable only with rigorous line-by-line review by qualified engineers, plus the same verification your domain already requires (formal review sign-off, traceability, extensive testing, and audits where applicable). For the most sensitive components, such as safety interlocks, cryptography, and core financial logic, consider level 1, and check whether your regulator or certification standard restricts AI-generated code."
    }
  },
  "summary": "Match review rigor to the cost of being wrong and to how long the code will live. Vibe code freely when the code is disposable, shift to selective review plus tests once real users or demos depend on it, and require full line-by-line human review once a team must maintain the code or failures carry serious consequences. Across all stages, the person who merges AI-written code owns it as if they wrote it, and code from an earlier stage should be reviewed to the new stage's standard (or rewritten) before it moves up."
}

